Task 1: Configure DNS Services for IdM in /etc/named.conf
Subtask 1.1: Examine Current DNS Configuration
First, let's examine the existing DNS configuration to understand the current setup.

Connect to the IdM server:
ssh root@idm-server
Check if BIND DNS service is installed:
rpm -qa | grep bind
systemctl status named
Examine the current named.conf file:
cat /etc/named.conf
Create a backup of the original configuration:
cp /etc/named.conf /etc/named.conf.backup
Subtask 1.2: Configure Basic DNS Settings
Edit the named.conf file:
vi /etc/named.conf
Add the following configuration (replace existing content if necessary):
//
// named.conf for IdM integration
//
options {
    listen-on port 53 { 127.0.0.1; 192.168.1.10; };
    listen-on-v6 port 53 { ::1; };
    directory       "/var/named";
    dump-file       "/var/named/data/cache_dump.db";
    statistics-file "/var/named/data/named_stats.txt";
    memstatistics-file "/var/named/data/named_mem_stats.txt";
    secroots-file   "/var/named/data/named.secroots";
    recursing-file  "/var/named/data/named.recursing";
    allow-query     { localhost; 192.168.1.0/24; };
    allow-recursion { localhost; 192.168.1.0/24; };
    
    dnssec-enable yes;
    dnssec-validation yes;
    
    bindkeys-file "/etc/named.root.key";
    
    managed-keys-directory "/var/named/dynamic";
    
    pid-file "/run/named/named.pid";
    session-keyfile "/run/named/session.key";
    
    forwarders {
        8.8.8.8;
        8.8.4.4;
    };
    forward only;
};

logging {
    channel default_debug {
        file "data/named.run";
        severity dynamic;
    };
};

zone "." IN {
    type hint;
    file "named.ca";
};

include "/etc/named.rfc1912.zones";
include "/etc/named.root.key";

// IdM will manage these zones dynamically
include "/etc/named/ipa-ext.conf";
include "/etc/named/ipa-options-ext.conf";
Set proper permissions:
chown root:named /etc/named.conf
chmod 640 /etc/named.conf
Subtask 1.3: Verify DNS Configuration Syntax
Check configuration syntax:
named-checkconf /etc/named.conf
If no errors are reported, start the named service:
systemctl enable named
systemctl start named
systemctl status named
Check if DNS is listening on the correct ports:
netstat -tulpn | grep :53
Task 2: Integrate DNS with IdM using ipa dnsconfig-mod
Subtask 2.1: Verify IdM Server Status
Check IdM server status:
ipactl status
If IdM is not running, start it:
ipactl start
Authenticate as IdM administrator:
kinit admin
Subtask 2.2: Configure DNS Integration Settings
View current DNS configuration in IdM:
ipa dnsconfig-show
Configure DNS forwarders for external resolution:
ipa dnsconfig-mod --forwarder=8.8.8.8 --forwarder=8.8.4.4
Enable DNS forwarding:
ipa dnsconfig-mod --forward-policy=only
Configure DNS search domains:
ipa dnsconfig-mod --allow-sync-ptr=TRUE
Subtask 2.3: Create DNS Zones in IdM
Create forward DNS zone:
ipa dnszone-add example.com --admin-email=admin@example.com
Create reverse DNS zone:
ipa dnszone-add 1.168.192.in-addr.arpa --admin-email=admin@example.com
Verify zones were created:
ipa dnszone-find
Subtask 2.4: Add DNS Records
Add A record for IdM server:
ipa dnsrecord-add example.com idm-server --a-rec=192.168.1.10
Add PTR record for reverse resolution:
ipa dnsrecord-add 1.168.192.in-addr.arpa 10 --ptr-rec=idm-server.example.com.
Add SRV records for IdM services:
ipa dnsrecord-add example.com _ldap._tcp --srv-rec="0 100 389 idm-server.example.com."
ipa dnsrecord-add example.com _kerberos._tcp --srv-rec="0 100 88 idm-server.example.com."
ipa dnsrecord-add example.com _kerberos._udp --srv-rec="0 100 88 idm-server.example.com."
Verify DNS records:
ipa dnsrecord-find example.com
Task 3: Verify DNS Resolution for IdM Servers and Clients
Subtask 3.1: Test DNS Resolution on IdM Server
Test forward DNS resolution:
nslookup idm-server.example.com localhost
Test reverse DNS resolution:
nslookup 192.168.1.10 localhost
Test SRV record resolution:
dig @localhost _ldap._tcp.example.com SRV
Test external DNS forwarding:
nslookup google.com localhost
Subtask 3.2: Configure IdM Client DNS Settings
Connect to the IdM client machine:
ssh root@idm-client
Configure client to use IdM server as DNS:
vi /etc/resolv.conf
Add the following content:
search example.com
nameserver 192.168.1.10
nameserver 8.8.8.8
Make the configuration persistent:
chattr +i /etc/resolv.conf
Subtask 3.3: Test DNS Resolution from Client
Test IdM server resolution from client:
nslookup idm-server.example.com
Test reverse resolution from client:
nslookup 192.168.1.10
Test SRV record resolution from client:
dig _ldap._tcp.example.com SRV
Verify client can resolve external domains:
nslookup google.com
Subtask 3.4: Advanced DNS Verification
Use systemd-resolve to check DNS status (if available):
systemd-resolve --status
Test DNS with specific query types:
dig @192.168.1.10 example.com SOA
dig @192.168.1.10 example.com NS
dig @192.168.1.10 idm-server.example.com A
Check DNS cache:
rndc dumpdb -cache
cat /var/named/data/cache_dump.db | grep example.com
Troubleshooting Common Issues
DNS Service Issues
Problem: Named service fails to start Solution:

# Check configuration syntax
named-checkconf /etc/named.conf

# Check system logs
journalctl -u named -f

# Verify file permissions
ls -la /etc/named.conf
IdM DNS Integration Issues
Problem: DNS zones not appearing in IdM Solution:

# Restart IdM services
ipactl restart

# Check IdM logs
tail -f /var/log/ipaserver-install.log

# Verify DNS plugin is loaded
ipa plugins-show dns
Client Resolution Issues
Problem: Client cannot resolve IdM records Solution:

# Check network connectivity
ping 192.168.1.10

# Verify DNS server is responding
nslookup idm-server.example.com 192.168.1.10

# Check firewall settings
firewall-cmd --list-all
